From b8f4e630e4f06eed17ba9302b74f54fb941bedd7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(camera): resolve server presets and camera movement

Protocol 2193 carries independent optional camera fields, including named
spline references, little-endian signed actor IDs and string-encoded FOV
and keyframe easing. Decode every instruction field before applying the
fade, retain the decoded instruction for integrations, and bound spline
allocations. Resolve named references through the received spline table.

Resolve overlapping fades in core and send versioned timeline snapshots
to subscribed clients after joining and channel registration. Late
subscribers receive elapsed state instead of restarting the effect. Keep
ordinary Java clients connected when no native rendering client subscribes.

The matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 server emitted seven captured instructions.
Its executable's fade insertion and updater confirm minimum duration,
current-opacity continuity, retained color and later fade-out completion.
Targeted tests compare native schedules and samples without distributing
native binaries, assets or captures.

Resolve FOV clamping, easing, repeated commands and clear lifecycle with a
shared core transition model. Transport elapsed snapshots and live commands
to subscribed rendering clients. Retain the local projection symbolically
when core lacks client context. Live commands capture the rendered client
projection; clear captures its normal projection. Native 1.26.51.1 updater
and easing probes supply private comparison fixtures for targeted tests.

Decode CameraShake packet 159 and preserve separate concurrent queues,
summed intensity capped at four, overlap decay and global stop. Share the
resumable queue and continuous simplex sampler with rendering clients.
Keep the queues current without a subscriber and send sequenced commands
and snapshots through ordinary Java payloads, including ViaProxy.

The pinned 1.26.51.1 executable and an eight-packet native capture establish
the codec, lifecycle and world-space position versus pitch/yaw rotation.
Private execution probes compare queue updates and authored noise inputs.
Live native camera arguments confirm frequency 10, amplitude five degrees
in radians and noise multiplier four. No proprietary assets are embedded.

Decode the complete protocol-2193 preset table, preserve wire indexes,
and resolve optional fields through the nearest declared ancestors.
Keep false and zero overrides, whole aim-assist objects, and each child's
starting-rotation flags distinct from ordinary inheritance. Resolve only
received built-in roots; report missing parents, cycles and unknown roots
without inventing free cameras. Reject duplicate names atomically.

Matching local, CubeCraft and authored custom captures establish three
different preset tables and byte-identical independent codec results.
The pinned client resolver at 1409f28e0 establishes ordinary inheritance.
Tests cover forward references, maximum depth, malformed counts and
optional overrides. Rendering, activation, controls and late camera pose
transport remain incomplete; retaining presets does not claim parity.

Resolve free-camera position, rotation, facing and per-preset instruction
overrides in core. Default clears position and rotation overrides. Deliver
a versioned resolved command to subscribed clients on both connection
routes; the rendering client supplies the interrupted frame pose. Decode
and retain unsupported fields without claiming their rendered behavior.

Port the target build's incremental blend updater, quaternion/Euler
conversion, shortest yaw arc, parent displacement and overshoot. The
private executable oracle matches 4,608 samples across all 32 eases.
Preset-instance command handling follows 1.26.51.1 function 1466f1230,
with blend arithmetic from 146786500 and pose conversion from 1466f7680.
The native capture contains inherited free presets and perspective sets.

Late subscribers receive the resolved settled target. Reconstructing an
interrupted transition requires the original client's rendered frame,
which core cannot invent. Follow/orbit, splines, actor tracking, controls,
effect/listener application and blend FOV coupling remain incomplete.

Execute free-camera entity tracking with independent preset instances, native
angular steps, rotation limits, radius checks, snap rearming and target
removal. Keep target generations separate from position blends and preserve
inactive rotations, explicit default resets and actor lifetime ownership.
Transport bounded preset and target snapshots to the rendering client.

Target 1.26.51.1 functions 1466fe8c0, 1466fe310, 14677b940 and
1466f7fd0 establish tracking, quaternion stepping and initialization.
Private execution comparisons cover 960 angular-step samples and 36
initialization samples. Full frame and visual parity remain separate gates.

Preserve the native priority when optional camera actions share a packet.
Target and removal win over clear without restarting the position blend;
attachment, detachment and spline actions cannot fall through to clear.
Use the same selection for FOV commands and resets, so a losing clear
cannot change projection while the target action preserves position.
Private execution of the target build's dispatch instructions compares
864 combinations, including absent and false flags. This establishes
selection only; boom-camera and spline execution remain incomplete.

Add the shared native orbit component model for follow-camera integration.
Keep radius smoothing relative to the previous world-space camera and the
current pivot, preserve wrapped angular springs and input inversion, and
apply a supplied collision radius directly. The target follow_orbit
profile sets all three spring rates to zero, unlike constructor defaults.

Private execution of the pinned native updater compares 270 chained
frames and 120 input updates. These probes use authored component state
and host CRT math boundaries. Actor binding, collision queries, protocol
transport and rendered follow-camera execution remain incomplete.

Transport follow-orbit preset instances and attachment generations on a
separate negotiated channel. Gate the new position mode on that channel
so older rendering clients never receive an unknown mode. Retain view and
entity offsets per preset without restarting position blends on attach.

Execute follow frames with the shared orbit model, native attachment and
view transforms, and look-at orientation. Bind explicit targets to actor
lifetimes and keep inactive preset state current. Carry the protocol
player-origin offset from core instead of substituting Java eye height.
Private component comparisons cover 256 composed frames. Full native
actor-pivot, input, avoidance and visible-rendering parity remain open.

Add the shared native avoidance updater for follow-camera integration.
Preserve eight offset corner casts, ordered weighted feelers on both sides
of each axis, immediate corner contraction and spring relaxation. Keep
native minimum-distance boundaries, optional seat spring overrides, and
actor-state collapse separate from the position blend.

Private execution compares 2,400 chained native frames and 14,400 ray
queries, including fixed-boom cache preparation, minimum expansion,
recovery and spring overrides. Actual native definition construction and
assignment establish empty default feelers and the pinned follow profile's
zero spring and quarter-block minimum. Host ray and CRT boundaries are
explicit. Native world clipping, actor-state lookup, projection inputs and
full frame scheduling remain integration requirements.

Add shared camera ray traversal and box surface intersections with native
float arithmetic, cell and face tie ordering, the direction threshold,
inside exits and the 200-step limit. Keep world geometry and policy in the
caller; fluid transitions and renderer bindings remain incomplete.

Private target execution compares 1,800 box segments and 506 world rays,
including all 2,688 sampled cells. Execute native overlay-air fallback,
filter dispatch, the common bounds wrapper and custom block intersection;
supply authored block geometry and explicit environment boundaries.

Run avoidance in follow frames around the attachment and view-offset
pivot. Use the native camera definition's 0.025 near plane as clearance
and sample solid-state collapse at the subject origin. Preserve the
pre-avoidance orientation and independent inactive preset instances.

Extend world tracing with base and extra layers, liquid material
transitions, auxiliary-fluid exclusion, and separate eligibility and
final hit bounds. Native custom blocks gate on collision envelopes but
intersect selection bounds. Private target execution compares 810 world
rays with every layer lookup and 768 composed follow-avoidance frames.
World geometry, actual type-specific policies, actor origin lookup and
complete ECS scheduling remain separate native parity requirements.

Execute inline spline programs in each free-camera instance before the
active camera blend. Keep spline commands independent of perspective
selection, share immutable curve geometry between instances, and transport
elapsed programs only through their negotiated channel. Preserve the last
pose when a frame exceeds the duration, without snapping to the endpoint.

Target-build functions 1466ef950, 1466fe120, 1466fd0c0 and 1466fda20
establish instruction compilation, channel timing and pose application.
Curve factories/evaluators 14677c280, 14677c8a0, 14677cb80 and 14677cd50
establish chord-weighted segments, endpoint tangents and minimum counts.
Private probes compare 26 factory cases, 220 curve samples and 84 composed
playback samples. Preserve native float operation order in Hermite sums.
Full lifecycle snapshots, complete ECS ordering and official-client
rendered parity remain separate requirements.

Decode CameraSpline packet 338 and replace the retained definition table
without mutating an already running program. Resolve the first exact
identifier match and preserve per-channel inherited easing, case-insensitive
ASCII curve types, and the definition's own time and channel values.

Correct required spline instruction type, identifier and load fields. The
old optional identifier decoder disconnected a matching 1.26.51.1 client
on play_spline. A target-server capture establishes the 149-byte definition
packet and named instruction layout, including optional named frame easing.
Native handler 14567da90 confirms replacement/shrink/empty transitions;
compiler 1466f05c0 supplies 36 lookup/compilation cases and 56 composed poses.
Keep those numeric results separate from full native system/render parity.

Preserve explicit position command revisions per free-camera preset so
repeating coordinates after a spline replaces the retained endpoint.
The pinned native SET handler writes these overrides without a value
comparison. Omitted position commands leave other instances untouched;
default resets also advance the revision. Negotiate tracking version two
on its own channel so older add-ons cannot misread the extended payload.
A live target-server sequence exposed the missing revision, and a shared
controller/runtime regression covers clear, repeat, inactive and default.

Retain native spline channels when another command arrives before playback
retires. Replace the curve and duration, append authored keyframes without
sorting, and restart time. This includes empty replacement channels and
mixed named/inline commands. Snapshots carry the complete retained program;
live commands compose against each camera instance's actual playback state.
Negotiate the command/snapshot distinction on a new spline channel.

Native compiler, initialization, updater and pose application probes cover
64 mixed replacements and 12 strict-duration retirement cases, including
shared-pointer release and ECS removal boundaries. Fresh empty channels
reach invalid target-build reads, so reject them safely. Bound cumulative
keyframe counts before allocation and reject composition atomically.

Release applied command payloads and retired instance programs. The core
connection tick releases expired snapshot channel history using its clock;
a deterministic clock test covers equality, strict expiry and a fresh
request. Full native ECS scheduling, queue/pause clock parity, terminal
pose recovery for late subscribers and visible parity remain unverified.
